  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nEarly afternoon analysis showed the cutoff that's been hanging \naround the last few days still spinning over MN. Moisture was \nwrapping around the back side and bringing some spotty light \nshowers and sprinkles to portions of the area, with the most \ncoverage in northeast NE into west-central IA in vicinity of a \nsurface trough. This area of precip will slowly meander \nsoutheast through the night and into Thursday as the low \nadvances eastward and drags the surface trough/cold front \nthrough the area. Precip won't amount to much, maybe a few \nhundredths of an inch at most, though could see some patchy \ndrizzle with reduced visibility in some spots early Thursday \nmorning. Precip should exit to the south by Thursday afternoon, \nthough clouds will linger across most of the area through the \nday. Expect highs to once again be in the mid 40s to mid 50s.\n\nSurface ridging will build in Thursday night into Friday \nmorning, with winds becoming very light. This should help \ntemperatures to tank overnight, but latest guidance suggests \nwe'll have some lingering clouds in the area, so maybe not quite\nas cold as previously thought. Still, portions of northeast NE \nlook to bottom out below freezing. Shortwave upper level ridging\nwill slide through during the day Friday, with the clouds \ngradually clearing out as it does so. This sets the stage for a \nmore widespread freeze Friday night into Saturday. While these \ntemperatures are very common for this time of year, given how \nwarm it has been, it's worth reminding you to disconnect outdoor\nhoses and bring in any plants you want to keep around for a \nlittle longer.\n\nSoutherly low level flow returns on Saturday as the surface \nhigh pushes to our east east. This will usher in warmer air, \nwith highs in the 50s to around 60 on Saturday and widespread \n60s Sunday into next week. Precipitation chances also look \npretty minimal through the remainder of the forecast. Guidance \nis in good agreement that a shortwave trough pushes through \nFriday night into Saturday, but is also in good agreement that \nthere will be no associated precip with lots of low level dry \nair and the surface high in the area. After that, we'll remain \non the eastern periphery of larger scale upper level ridging \nover the western CONUS with a vast majority of ensemble members \nkeeping any additional shortwaves and precip out of our area.",
